<BaseLayout title="Native America Today | People, Nations & Achievements" description="Meet Indigenous scientists, artists, writers, athletes, veterans, and political leaders shaping contemporary America.">
<section class="hero"><div class="wrap"><p class="eyebrow">INDIGENOUS PEOPLES · CONTEMPORARY AMERICA</p><h1>Native America Today</h1><h2>Beyond the Westerns. Beyond the Past.</h2><p class="lead">Meet the people, nations, ideas, and achievements shaping the United States today. Indigenous history did not end in the nineteenth century. Native people are scientists, engineers, writers, artists, athletes, veterans, elected officials, and leaders of sovereign Tribal Nations.</p><p class="ru">Познакомьтесь с людьми, народами, идеями и достижениями, которые формируют современную Америку. История коренных народов не закончилась в XIX веке. Сегодня это учёные, инженеры, писатели, художники, спортсмены, ветераны, избранные представители власти и руководители самостоятельных племенных наций.</p><nav><a href="#science">Science</a><a href="#arts">Arts & Literature</a><a href="#sports">Sports</a><a href="#war">World War II</a><a href="#leaders">Leadership</a></nav></div></section>
<main class="wrap content"><a class="back" href="/american-english-method/learn/history-heritage/native-peoples/">← Indigenous Peoples of North America</a>
<section class="intro"><p class="eyebrow">A DIFFERENT WAY TO SEE AMERICA</p><h2>Not one story. Many living nations.</h2><p>For generations, popular books and films presented Native Americans mainly through frontier stories: cowboys, battles, reservations, and a distant past. Those stories are only a small and often distorted window. Contemporary Indigenous life includes research laboratories, universities, professional sports, film sets, public institutions, Tribal governments, businesses, and community-led cultural work.</p><p>There is no single Native American experience. The people featured here belong to different nations and communities, have different careers and viewpoints, and live in different parts of the country. Their achievements should be understood in their own contexts — not treated as symbols of one supposedly uniform culture.</p><p class="ru">Популярная литература и кино долго показывали коренных американцев преимущественно через сюжеты о фронтире и далёком прошлом. Но современная жизнь гораздо шире: лаборатории, университеты, космические программы, спорт, кино, государственные учреждения и собственные правительства племенных народов. У каждого человека и каждого народа — своя история.</p></section>

<section class="feature" id="science"><header><span>01 · SCIENCE, TECHNOLOGY & MEDICINE</span><h2>Scientists and innovators</h2><p>Indigenous professionals contribute to aerospace engineering, planetary exploration, environmental science, hydrology, and medicine.</p></header><div class="grid">
<figure class="person-photo portrait-full"><img src="https://assets-global.website-files.com/641c660b575cce2aff55713e/6594831b6e8c932c020ddcc6_ROSS%2C%20MARY%20GOLDA.jpg" alt="Mary Golda Ross standing beside two scale rocket models" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Mary Golda Ross with rocket models · Oklahoma Hall of Fame</figcaption></figure><article><label>AEROSPACE ENGINEERING</label><h3>Mary Golda Ross</h3><b>Cherokee Nation</b><p>Mathematician and aerospace engineer, and the first known Native American woman engineer. At Lockheed, she became a founding member of the highly classified Skunk Works program. Her work contributed to aerospace design and early concepts for satellites and spaceflight.</p><p class="ru">Математик и аэрокосмический инженер, первая известная женщина-инженер из числа коренных американцев. Работала в Lockheed и участвовала в секретной программе Skunk Works.</p><a href="https://home.nps.gov/people/mary-g-ross.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">National Park Service · Biography ↗</a></article>
<figure class="person-photo portrait-full"><img src="https://static.wixstatic.com/media/b4fc12_f9787aba4793420798ed43417d8792f3~mv2.png/v1/fill/w_980%2Ch_552%2Cal_c%2Cq_90%2Cusm_0.66_1.00_0.01%2Cenc_auto/b4fc12_f9787aba4793420798ed43417d8792f3~mv2.png" alt="Diné mechanical engineer Aaron Yazzie in a portrait profile graphic" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Aaron Yazzie, Diné mechanical engineer · Image: National Native Development and Digital Council</figcaption></figure><article><label>PLANETARY ENGINEERING</label><h3>Aaron Yazzie</h3><b>Diné (Navajo Nation)</b><p>A mechanical engineer at NASA’s Jet Propulsion Laboratory, Aaron Yazzie has worked on systems used in Mars exploration, including Curiosity and Perseverance. He also encourages Indigenous young people to see themselves in science and space exploration.</p><p class="ru">Инженер-механик NASA, участвовавший в разработке и испытаниях механизмов для марсоходов Curiosity и Perseverance. Он также помогает молодёжи из коренных общин увидеть возможности научной карьеры.</p><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/learning-resources/bridging-indigenous-heritage-and-space-exploration/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NASA · Indigenous Heritage and Space Exploration ↗</a></article>
<figure class="person-photo"><img src="https://superfund.arizona.edu/sites/default/files/Karletta%20Chief%20Highlight.jpg" alt="Dr. Karletta Chief, Diné environmental scientist" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Dr. Karletta Chief · Photo: University of Arizona</figcaption></figure><article><label>HYDROLOGY & ENVIRONMENTAL SCIENCE</label><h3>Dr. Karletta Chief</h3><b>Diné (Navajo Nation)</b><p>Professor of environmental science and hydrology at the University of Arizona and director of its Indigenous Resilience Center. Her research and outreach address water resources, environmental impacts, climate resilience, and food, energy, and water security in partnership with Native Nations.</p><p class="ru">Профессор экологических наук и гидрологии Университета Аризоны. Исследует водные ресурсы, загрязнение, изменение климата и устойчивость коренных общин.</p><a href="https://profiles.arizona.edu/person/kchief" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">University of Arizona · Faculty profile ↗</a></article>
</div><div class="profiles-list" aria-label="Additional Indigenous scientists and medical professionals">
<article><img class="profile-image" src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8f/BegayFred.jpg" alt="Physicist Fred Begay in front of a Navajo cultural display" loading="lazy"><p class="image-credit">Fred Begay · LeRoy N. Sanchez / Los Alamos National Laboratory · Public domain</p><label>NUCLEAR PHYSICS</label><h3>Dr. Fred Begay</h3><b>Diné and Ute heritage</b><p>A nuclear physicist who earned a Ph.D. from the University of New Mexico and worked at Los Alamos National Laboratory on laser research and thermonuclear fusion. He also advised and mentored Native communities in science and education.</p><a href="https://science.gsfc.nasa.gov/attic/sunearthday.nasa.gov/2005/na/bio_fred.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NASA · Fred Begay biography ↗</a> <a href="https://www.osti.gov/servlets/purl/1679987" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Los Alamos National Laboratory · Profile ↗</a></article>
<article><img class="profile-image" src="https://newsroom.asu.edu/sites/g/files/litvpz3676/files/2026-10/20261009%20TsosieLabAndHerbariumatAlameda_057.jpg" alt="Geneticist Krystal Tsosie in a university garden" loading="lazy"><p class="image-credit">Krystal Tsosie · Arizona State University · University-provided photo</p><label>GENETICS & BIOETHICS</label><h3>Dr. Krystal Tsosie</h3><b>Diné (Navajo Nation)</b><p>A geneticist and bioethicist whose work addresses population genetics, precision health, Indigenous data sovereignty, and ethical partnerships between researchers and Native communities. She is a co-founder of the Native BioData Consortium.</p><a href="https://www.ou.edu/cas/centers/ethics-of-indigenous-genetic-research/people" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">University of Oklahoma · Indigenous genetic research ↗</a></article>
<article><label>BIOMEDICAL INFORMATICS & GENOMICS</label><h3>Dr. Katrina Claw</h3><b>Diné (Navajo Nation)</b><p>An associate professor at the University of Colorado Anschutz Medical Campus. Her research explores pharmacogenomics, personalized medicine, and the ethical and cultural implications of genomic research with Indigenous communities. She received the 2024 NSF Alan T. Waterman Award.</p><a href="https://som.cuanschutz.edu/Profiles/Faculty/Profile/28794" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">University of Colorado · Faculty profile ↗</a></article>
<article><label>AEROSPACE ENGINEERING · NASA</label><h3>Dr. Powtawche Valerino</h3><b>Mississippi Choctaw</b><p>An aerospace engineer at NASA Marshall Space Flight Center. Her work has included guidance, navigation, and mission analysis for major spaceflight programs, including the Space Launch System and Artemis. She also worked on the Cassini-Huygens, Europa Clipper, and Parker Solar Probe missions.</p><a href="https://www.nasa.gov/people-of-nasa/special-observances/native-american-heritage-month/powtawche-valerino/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NASA · Powtawche Valerino ↗</a> <a href="https://nacc.stanford.edu/50-50-dr-powtawche-valerino" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Stanford · Profile ↗</a></article>
<article><img class="profile-image" src="https://www.astria.health/cache/img/c/8/3/7/8/c83789e05cea9c7ee952bba5b3546500.jpg" alt="Surgeon Dr. Lori Arviso Alvord in a professional portrait" loading="lazy"><p class="image-credit">Dr. Lori Arviso Alvord · Astria Health provider portrait</p><label>SURGERY & MEDICAL EDUCATION</label><h3>Dr. Lori Arviso Alvord</h3><b>Diné (Navajo Nation)</b><p>A Stanford-trained surgeon and the first Navajo woman to become board-certified in surgery. Her work and writing explore how conventional medicine can engage respectfully with Navajo understandings of healing and the whole person.</p><a href="https://www.nlm.nih.gov/exhibition/changing-the-face-of-medicine/physicians/biography_lori_alvord.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. National Library of Medicine · Biography ↗</a></article>
</div><aside><b>Why this matters:</b> These careers are part of the present — and help younger generations imagine their own futures in science and technology.</aside></section>

<section class="feature" id="arts"><header><span>02 · LITERATURE, FILM & VISUAL ARTS</span><h2>Writers, artists, and storytellers</h2><p>Contemporary Native creativity does more than preserve tradition: it experiments, challenges stereotypes, and speaks to the wider world.</p></header><div class="grid">
<figure class="person-photo"><img src="https://assets.vogue.com/photos/5d0a48bfd4c3e94474210ce4/16%3A9/w_2000%2Ch_1125%2Cc_limit/promo-joy-harjo.jpg" alt="Poet and musician Joy Harjo" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Joy Harjo · Portrait via <a href="https://www.vogue.com/article/joy-harjo-first-native-american-woman-us-poet-laureate" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Vogue</a></figcaption></figure><article><label>POETRY & MUSIC</label><h3>Joy Harjo</h3><b>Muscogee (Creek) Nation</b><p>Poet, musician, performer, and author, Joy Harjo served three terms as the 23rd Poet Laureate of the United States (2019–2022). Her work draws on Muscogee history, Native storytelling, memory, music, and contemporary Indigenous experience. She was the first Native American U.S. Poet Laureate.</p><p class="ru">Поэтесса, музыкант и писательница. В 2019–2022 годах была 23-м поэтом-лауреатом США — первой представительницей коренных народов на этой должности.</p><a href="https://www.poetryfoundation.org/articles/141911/joy-harjo-selections" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Poetry Foundation · Joy Harjo ↗</a></article>
<figure class="person-photo"><img src="https://media.newyorker.com/photos/590950dfc14b3c606c10359d/master/pass/erdrich-580.jpg" alt="Novelist Louise Erdrich" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Louise Erdrich · Portrait: Ulf Andersen/Getty via <a href="https://www.newyorker.com/books/double-take/letter-from-the-archive-louise-erdrich" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The New Yorker</a></figcaption></figure><article><label>NOVELS & FICTION</label><h3>Louise Erdrich</h3><b>Turtle Mountain Band of Chippewa Indians</b><p>An acclaimed novelist, poet, and bookseller whose fiction explores family, community, memory, history, and contemporary Native life. Her novel <em>The Night Watchman</em> won the 2021 Pulitzer Prize for Fiction.</p><p class="ru">Известная писательница и поэтесса. Её роман <em>The Night Watchman</em> получил Пулитцеровскую премию 2021 года. В её книгах история оджибве раскрывается через судьбы семей и сообществ.</p><a href="https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poets/louise-erdrich" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Poetry Foundation · Louise Erdrich ↗</a></article>
<figure class="person-photo"><img src="https://www.saic.edu/sites/default/files/styles/max_1300x1300/public/2023-07/27venice-bienniale1-wqpg-superjumbo.jpg?itok=hhHdjwgq" alt="Artist Jeffrey Gibson" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Jeffrey Gibson · Photo: School of the Art Institute of Chicago</figcaption></figure><article><label>CONTEMPORARY ART</label><h3>Jeffrey Gibson</h3><b>Mississippi Band of Choctaw Indians; of Cherokee descent</b><p>A multidisciplinary artist combining painting, sculpture, video, installation, beadwork, and other materials. In 2024, he represented the United States at the Venice Biennale with <em>the space in which to place me</em> — the first solo presentation by an Indigenous artist in the U.S. Pavilion.</p><p class="ru">Художник, работающий с живописью, скульптурой, видео и инсталляциями. В 2024 году представлял США на Венецианской биеннале с первой персональной выставкой коренного художника в американском павильоне.</p><a href="https://americanindian.si.edu/nk360/jeffrey-gibson/biography/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Smithsonian · Jeffrey Gibson ↗</a></article>
<figure class="person-photo"><img src="https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/5efce5920d28887981c5bd9b/58b8f71d-c019-4951-a9a7-fc43fcb194b6/Sterlin%2BHarjo.jpeg" alt="Filmmaker Sterlin Harjo" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Sterlin Harjo · Portrait via <a href="https://www.blackfilmandtv.com/news/array-releasing-acquires-love-and-fury-from-award-winning-indigenous-filmmaker-sterlin-harjo" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">BlackFilmandTV</a></figcaption></figure><article><label>FILM & TELEVISION</label><h3>Sterlin Harjo</h3><b>Seminole Nation of Oklahoma; Muscogee descendant</b><p>Writer, director, and producer who co-created <em>Reservation Dogs</em>, a series following Indigenous teenagers in rural Oklahoma. Its humour, contemporary setting, and Native writers, actors, directors, and crew offer a view of Native life rarely found in older Westerns.</p><p class="ru">Сценарист, режиссёр и продюсер, один из создателей сериала <em>Reservation Dogs</em> о современных подростках из коренного сообщества Оклахомы.</p><a href="https://www.amacad.org/person/sterlin-b-harjo" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">American Academy of Arts and Sciences ↗</a></article>
<figure class="person-photo portrait-full"><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/93/Lily_G.png" alt="Actor Lily Gladstone" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Lily Gladstone at Cannes, 2023 · Photo: Mrikkk / Wikimedia Commons · CC0</figcaption></figure><article><label>ACTING</label><h3>Lily Gladstone</h3><b>Blackfeet and Nez Perce Tribal Nations</b><p>An award-winning actor whose performance as Mollie Burkhart in <em>Killers of the Flower Moon</em> brought international attention. She became the first Native American nominated for the Academy Award for Best Actress and won the 2024 Golden Globe for Best Actress in a Motion Picture – Drama.</p><p class="ru">Актриса из народов Blackfeet и Nez Perce. Роль Молли Беркхарт принесла ей международное признание и историческую номинацию на «Оскар» за лучшую женскую роль.</p><a href="https://www.festival-cannes.com/en/p/lily-gladstone/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Festival de Cannes · Lily Gladstone ↗</a></article>
</div><div class="profiles-list" aria-label="Additional Indigenous visual artists">
<article><img class="profile-image" src="https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/651b5cc87f99cd16afd0df63/5957d1f7-645b-46c4-97b8-742ba195021e/Cara2020_selfiecasual_web_BW_Noise.jpg" alt="Contemporary photographer Cara Romero in a black-and-white portrait" loading="lazy"><p class="image-credit">Cara Romero · Artist’s official website</p><label>PHOTOGRAPHY & CONTEMPORARY STORYTELLING</label><h3>Cara Romero</h3><b>Chemehuevi Indian Tribe</b><p>A contemporary fine-art photographer whose vivid, carefully staged images challenge outdated representations of Native people. Her work explores cultural memory, Indigenous identity, and the realities of Native life today. She is an enrolled citizen of the Chemehuevi Indian Tribe.</p><a href="https://www.cararomero.com/about" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Artist’s official biography and statement ↗</a></article>
</div></section>

<section class="feature" id="sports"><header><span>03 · SPORT & INTERNATIONAL ACHIEVEMENT</span><h2>Athletes on the world stage</h2><p>From Olympic athletics to professional sport, Indigenous athletes have made their mark across generations.</p></header><div class="grid">
<figure class="person-photo portrait-full"><img src="https://olympedia-photos.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/79125.jpg" alt="Jim Thorpe in his 1912 Olympic-era athletic uniform" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Jim Thorpe · Historical photograph via <a href="https://www.olympedia.org/athletes/79125" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Olympedia</a></figcaption></figure><article><label>OLYMPIC LEGEND · TRACK & FIELD</label><h3>Jim Thorpe</h3><b>Sac and Fox Nation</b><p>At the 1912 Stockholm Olympics, Jim Thorpe won gold in both the pentathlon and decathlon. He later played professional baseball and football and became the first president of the American Professional Football Association, the forerunner of the NFL.</p><p class="ru">Джим Торп завоевал две золотые медали на Олимпийских играх 1912 года — в пятиборье и десятиборье. Позднее он играл в профессиональный бейсбол и американский футбол.</p><a href="https://usopm.org/jim-thorpe/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. Olympic & Paralympic Hall of Fame ↗</a></article>
<figure class="person-photo portrait-full"><img src="https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Billy_Mills%2C_1964.jpg/960px-Billy_Mills%2C_1964.jpg" alt="Billy Mills winning the 10,000 metres at the 1964 Tokyo Olympics" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Billy Mills, Los Angeles Times sports office, December 24, 1964 · UCLA Library / Los Angeles Times Photo Collection · CC BY 4.0</figcaption></figure><article><label>OLYMPIC GOLD · DISTANCE RUNNING</label><h3>Billy Mills</h3><b>Oglala Lakota</b><p>At the 1964 Tokyo Olympics, Billy Mills won the 10,000-metre race in a dramatic finish. He remains the only American man to have won Olympic gold in that event.</p><p class="ru">Билли Миллс выиграл бег на 10 000 метров на Олимпийских играх 1964 года в Токио. Он остаётся единственным американцем, завоевавшим олимпийское золото в этой дисциплине.</p><a href="https://www.teamusa.com/hall-of-fame/hall-of-fame-members/billy-mills" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Team USA · Billy Mills ↗</a></article>
<figure class="person-photo portrait-full"><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ed/Tewanima_%28winner%29%2C_Arquette_%28came_in_5th%29_LOC_2163457616.jpg/960px-Tewanima_%28winner%29%2C_Arquette_%28came_in_5th%29_LOC_2163457616.jpg" alt="Hopi runner Louis Tewanima, identified as the winner, with fellow Carlisle runner Mitchell Arquette after a New York City race, May 6, 1911" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Louis Tewanima (identified as the winner) and Mitchell Arquette, May 6, 1911 · Bain News Service / Library of Congress · No known restrictions on publication</figcaption></figure><article><label>OLYMPIC SILVER · DISTANCE RUNNING</label><h3>Louis Tewanima</h3><b>Hopi</b><p>Louis Tewanima won silver in the 10,000 metres at the 1912 Stockholm Olympics. His achievement came more than half a century before Billy Mills’s gold medal in the same event.</p><p class="ru">Луис Теванима, представитель народа хопи, завоевал серебряную медаль в беге на 10 000 метров на Олимпиаде 1912 года.</p><a href="https://www.nyrr.org/news/2025/11/03/notable-native-american-runners-in-history-and-today" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">New York Road Runners · Native runners ↗</a></article>
</div><div class="profiles-list" aria-label="Additional Indigenous athletes">
<article><label>PROFESSIONAL GOLF</label><h3>Notah Begay III</h3><b>Diné, San Felipe Pueblo, and Isleta Pueblo</b><p>A four-time PGA TOUR winner and golf analyst for NBC Sports and Golf Channel. He founded the NB3 Foundation, which supports Native American youth through health, wellness, and sports programs.</p><a href="https://nb3foundation.org/about/board-of-directors/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NB3 Foundation · Biography ↗</a></article>
<article><label>WOMEN’S BASKETBALL · WNBA</label><h3>Shoni Schimmel</h3><b>Confederated Tribes of the Umatilla Indian Reservation</b><p>A former WNBA guard and a major figure in Native women’s basketball. She was selected eighth overall in the 2014 WNBA Draft and became the first rookie to win the WNBA All-Star Game MVP award, scoring a record 29 points in that game.</p><a href="https://news.louisville.edu/news/native-american-basketball-fans-head-louisville" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">University of Louisville · Schimmel sisters ↗</a> <a href="https://www.museumofindigenouspeople.org/post/native-women-in-the-wnba" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Museum of Indigenous People · Native women in the WNBA ↗</a></article>
<article><img class="profile-image" src="https://static.www.nfl.com/image/upload/t_headshot_desktop/league/avv8nnqnrgv3os85v9zm" alt="Kansas City Chiefs center Creed Humphrey in his official NFL headshot" loading="lazy"><p class="image-credit">Creed Humphrey · NFL official player headshot</p><label>AMERICAN FOOTBALL · NFL</label><h3>Creed Humphrey</h3><b>Citizen Potawatomi Nation</b><p>An NFL center for the Kansas City Chiefs and a citizen of the Citizen Potawatomi Nation. He has spoken about the importance of representing his Nation and giving Native children a visible role model in professional sport.</p><a href="https://www.potawatomi.org/blog/2023/02/10/qa-with-kansas-city-chiefs-center-creed-humphrey/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Citizen Potawatomi Nation · Interview ↗</a></article>
<article><label>AMERICAN FOOTBALL · A NEW GENERATION</label><h3>Shiyazh Pete</h3><b>Navajo Nation; also of Salish heritage</b><p>An offensive tackle who became the first member of the Navajo Nation to appear in an NFL game, playing in preseason for the Dallas Cowboys in 2026. The Associated Press documented the welcome he received from Native fans and young athletes. His story is a milestone in representation, regardless of roster status.</p><a href="https://www.ap.org/news-highlights/spotlights/2026/shiyazh-pete-first-navajo-nfl-player-recognized-on-indigenous-peoples-day/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Associated Press · Shiyazh Pete ↗</a></article>
<article><label>WOMEN’S BASKETBALL · WNBA PIONEER</label><h3>Ryneldi Becenti</h3><b>Diné (Navajo Nation)</b><p>A trailblazing basketball player who became the first Native American woman to play in the WNBA, appearing for the Phoenix Mercury in the league’s inaugural 1997 season.</p><a href="https://www.ap.org/news-highlights/spotlights/2026/shiyazh-pete-first-navajo-nfl-player-recognized-on-indigenous-peoples-day/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Associated Press · Native athletes and milestones ↗</a></article>
<article><img class="profile-image" src="https://cdn.wnba.com/headshots/wnba/latest/1040x760/1642801.png" alt="WNBA player Aaliyah Nye in her official player headshot" loading="lazy"><p class="image-credit">Aaliyah Nye · Official WNBA player headshot</p><label>WOMEN’S BASKETBALL · WNBA</label><h3>Aaliyah Nye</h3><b>Match-E-Be-Nash-She-Wish Band of Pottawatomi</b><p>A professional basketball player who entered the WNBA in 2025. Her journey adds a contemporary chapter to the story of Native women in elite basketball and the visibility of Indigenous athletes in professional leagues.</p><a href="https://www.ap.org/news-highlights/spotlights/2026/shiyazh-pete-first-navajo-nfl-player-recognized-on-indigenous-peoples-day/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Associated Press · Native athletes and milestones ↗</a></article>
</div><aside><h3>Lacrosse and the Haudenosaunee</h3><p>Lacrosse has deep Indigenous roots. For Haudenosaunee peoples, the game carries cultural, community, and spiritual meaning as well as athletic importance. The Haudenosaunee Nationals represent their nations in international lacrosse. This is a powerful example of a traditional game becoming a modern international sport without losing its cultural significance.</p><p class="ru">Лакросс имеет глубокие корни в культурах коренных народов. Для хауденосауни это не только спорт, но и часть культурной и общественной жизни. Сборные Haudenosaunee Nationals представляют свои народы на международной арене.</p><a href="https://worldlacrosse.sport/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">World Lacrosse ↗</a></aside></section>

<section class="feature" id="war"><header><span>04 · COURAGE & SERVICE · 1939–1945</span><h2>Native American heroes of World War II</h2><p>Indigenous men and women served in the armed forces and supported the war effort at home. Their stories include military skill, sacrifice, language, and a complicated relationship with a country that had often denied Native communities their rights.</p></header><div class="stats"><div><strong>44,000</strong><span>American Indians served on active duty during World War II, according to the Smithsonian National Museum of the American Indian.</span></div><div><strong>Nearly 800</strong><span>Native American women also served in the U.S. armed forces.</span></div></div><div class="grid">
<figure class="person-photo"><img src="https://images.squarespace-cdn.com/content/v1/5843057debbd1a9265704e03/1623345923326-XY6MDHT822QYIYIX5TAR/CLOSEUP.jpg" alt="Navajo Code Talkers operating a field radio during World War II" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Navajo Code Talkers at a field radio · Photo via <a href="https://www.theattic.space/home-page-blogs/2018/5/24/when-i-met-the-code-talkers" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Attic</a></figcaption></figure><article><label>CODE TALKERS · PACIFIC</label><h3>The Navajo (Diné) Code Talkers</h3><b>Diné (Navajo Nation)</b><p>More than 400 Navajo Marines served as Code Talkers during World War II. They transmitted military messages using their language and a specially developed code. Their communications supported Marine operations across the Pacific, including Guadalcanal, Iwo Jima, and Okinawa. The code was never deciphered by Japanese forces; their work remained classified for decades.</p><p class="ru">Более 400 морских пехотинцев-навахо передавали военные сообщения на языке динé с использованием специально разработанного кодового словаря. Противнику не удалось расшифровать эти сообщения; официальное признание пришло спустя десятилетия.</p><a href="https://www.loc.gov/collections/veterans-history-project-collection/serving-our-voices/world-war-ii/navajo-code-talkers/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Library of Congress ↗</a><a href="https://www.history.navy.mil/research/library/online-reading-room/title-list-alphabetically/n/code-talkers.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. Navy ↗</a></article>
<figure class="person-photo"><img src="https://api.army.mil/e2/c/images/2012/10/31/270115/original.jpg" alt="Nine of the original Comanche Code Talkers; Charles Chibitty is seated in the front row, second from the left" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Nine of the original 17 Comanche Code Talkers · U.S. Army photograph, 2012</figcaption></figure><article><label>CODE TALKER · EUROPE</label><h3>Charles Chibitty</h3><b>Comanche Nation</b><p>Corporal Charles Chibitty was one of the Comanche Code Talkers who served with the U.S. Army in Europe. Comanche speakers transmitted messages that helped Allied forces communicate securely during the campaign, including the Normandy invasion.</p><p class="ru">Капрал Чарльз Чибитти был одним из команчей-кодировщиков в армии США. Их язык помогал союзникам передавать защищённые сообщения на европейском театре военных действий.</p><a href="https://www.okhistory.org/historycenter/militaryhof/inductee.php?id=122" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Oklahoma Military Hall of Fame ↗</a></article>
<figure class="person-photo"><img src="https://www.archives.gov/files/research/still-pictures/080-g-0413988-28-1357m.jpg" alt="U.S. Marines raising the American flag on Mount Suribachi, Iwo Jima, 1945" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Raising the Flag on Iwo Jima, February 23, 1945 · U.S. National Archives, Identifier 520748</figcaption></figure><article><label>MARINE · IWO JIMA</label><h3>Ira Hayes</h3><b>Akimel O’odham (Pima)</b><p>Ira Hayes was one of the six U.S. servicemen photographed raising the American flag on Mount Suribachi during the Battle of Iwo Jima on February 23, 1945. The photograph became one of the most recognizable images of the war. His life after that moment reminds us that public fame does not tell the whole story of a veteran’s experience.</p><p class="ru">Айра Хейс, представитель народа акимель-оодхам (пима), был одним из шести военнослужащих, поднявших американский флаг на горе Сурибати. Знаменитая фотография стала одним из символов войны.</p><a href="https://americanindian.si.edu/why-we-serve/topics/world-war-2/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Smithsonian · Why We Serve ↗</a></article>
<figure class="person-photo portrait-full"><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/88/Joe_Medicine_Crow_%28c._2009%29_%28cropped%29.jpg" alt="Joe Medicine Crow speaking in traditional regalia, circa 2009" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Joe Medicine Crow, c. 2009 · U.S. Department of the Interior · Public domain</figcaption></figure><article><label>WARRIOR · HISTORIAN</label><h3>Joseph Medicine Crow</h3><b>Apsáalooke (Crow Nation)</b><p>Joseph Medicine Crow served in the U.S. Army during World War II and later became an anthropologist, historian, and cultural ambassador for the Crow Nation. He was widely recognized as the last Crow war chief. His life links military service with the preservation of Apsáalooke history.</p><p class="ru">Джозеф Медисин Кроу служил в армии США во время Второй мировой войны, а позднее стал антропологом, историком и культурным представителем народа апсáалукэ (кроу).</p><a href="https://www.va.gov/cheyenne-health-care/programs/dr-joseph-medicine-crow-high-bird/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. Department of Veterans Affairs ↗</a></article>
<figure class="person-photo portrait-full"><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7d/Van_T._Barfoot.jpg" alt="Van T. Barfoot wearing a suit and his Medal of Honor" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Van T. Barfoot · U.S. Army photograph · Public domain</figcaption></figure><article><label>MEDAL OF HONOR · U.S. ARMY</label><h3>Van T. Barfoot</h3><b>Choctaw ancestry</b><p>Van T. Barfoot received the Medal of Honor for extraordinary actions near Carano, Italy, on May 23, 1944. The National Museum of the United States Army records that his maternal grandmother was a member of the Choctaw Nation. We distinguish documented family ancestry from a confirmed statement of Tribal citizenship.</p><p class="ru">Ван Т. Барфут получил Медаль Почёта за действия в Италии 23 мая 1944 года. Музей армии США указывает, что его бабушка по материнской линии принадлежала к народу чокто. Это подтверждает происхождение, но само по себе не доказывает гражданство племенной нации.</p><a href="https://www.thenmusa.org/biographies/van-t-barfoot/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">National Museum of the United States Army ↗</a></article>
</div><aside><b>A wider story:</b> Code Talkers came from many nations, not only the Navajo. Choctaw soldiers had used their language in World War I. During World War II, Comanche, Meskwaki, Chippewa, Oneida, Hopi, Cherokee, Creek, and other language communities also contributed. The historical record is incomplete, and not every participant is known by name.</aside><p class="source-line"><a href="https://americanindian.si.edu/why-we-serve/topics/code-talkers/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Smithsonian · Code Talkers ↗</a> · <a href="https://www.archives.gov/research/native-americans/military/code-talkers.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">National Archives · Code Talkers ↗</a></p></section>

<section class="feature" id="leaders"><header><span>05 · PUBLIC SERVICE & SELF-GOVERNMENT</span><h2>Political leaders and Tribal Nations</h2><p>Native political life takes place in more than one arena. Indigenous citizens serve in U.S. federal and state institutions; Tribal Nations also have their own governments, leaders, laws, and political priorities.</p></header><aside><h3>Two political arenas — one complex relationship</h3><p>Members of Native nations may be elected to the U.S. Congress, state legislatures, or governorships. Separately, Tribal Nations elect or appoint their own leaders and operate governments under their own constitutions, laws, and traditions. Tribal sovereignty is not the same as statehood, and Tribal governments are not simply cultural organizations. Their relationship with the United States is shaped by treaties, federal law, and a government-to-government framework.</p><p class="ru">Представители коренных народов могут избираться в Конгресс США и на посты губернаторов. Одновременно племенные нации имеют собственные правительства и руководителей. Это две разные, хотя и взаимодействующие политические системы.</p></aside><div class="grid">
<figure class="person-photo portrait-full"><img src="https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Deb_Haaland%2C_official_portrait%2C_116th_Congress.jpg/960px-Deb_Haaland%2C_official_portrait%2C_116th_Congress.jpg" alt="Deb Haaland in her official U.S. House portrait" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Deb Haaland · Official U.S. House portrait, 2018 · Public domain</figcaption></figure><article><label>GOVERNOR-ELECT · NEW MEXICO</label><h3>Deb Haaland</h3><b>Pueblo of Laguna</b><p>Elected Governor of New Mexico in November 2026, Deb Haaland is the first Native American woman elected governor of a U.S. state. She previously served in the U.S. House of Representatives and became the first Native American to lead the Department of the Interior as Secretary of the Interior (2021–2025). Her gubernatorial term is due to begin in January 2027.</p><p class="ru">Деб Хааланд избрана губернатором Нью-Мексико в ноябре 2026 года. Она станет первой женщиной из коренных народов на посту губернатора американского штата. Ранее была министром внутренних дел США.</p><a href="https://www.ncai.org/news/ncai-denounces-racist-portrayal-of-new-mexico-governor-elect-deb-haaland-in-albuquerque-journal-cartoon" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">National Congress of American Indians ↗</a></article>
<figure class="person-photo portrait-full"><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c6/2026PeggyFlanagan.jpg" alt="Peggy Flanagan, member of the White Earth Nation" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Peggy Flanagan, March 2026 · Photo: Travellers & Tinkers / Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 4.0</figcaption></figure><article><label>U.S. SENATOR-ELECT · MINNESOTA</label><h3>Peggy Flanagan</h3><b>White Earth Nation (Ojibwe)</b><p>Elected to the U.S. Senate from Minnesota in 2026, Peggy Flanagan is set to become the first Native American woman to serve in the Senate when the 120th Congress begins in January 2027. Before her election, she served as Lieutenant Governor of Minnesota.</p><p class="ru">Пегги Фланаган избрана в Сенат США от Миннесоты. После начала работы нового Конгресса в январе 2027 года она станет первой женщиной из коренных народов в Сенате.</p><a href="https://www.ncai.org/news/seven120thcongress" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NCAI · 120th Congress ↗</a></article>
<figure class="person-photo portrait-full"><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Tom_cole.jpg/960px-Tom_cole.jpg" alt="Tom Cole in his official congressional portrait" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Tom Cole · Official U.S. congressional portrait · Public domain</figcaption></figure><article><label>U.S. REPRESENTATIVE</label><h3>Tom Cole</h3><b>Chickasaw Nation</b><p>A citizen of the Chickasaw Nation and a long-serving member of the U.S. House of Representatives from Oklahoma. His public career has included work on federal budgets and Native American affairs.</p><p class="ru">Том Коул — гражданин Chickasaw Nation и многолетний член Палаты представителей США от Оклахомы. В его работе важное место занимают федеральный бюджет и вопросы коренных народов.</p><a href="https://www.ncai.org/news/seven120thcongress" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NCAI · 120th Congress ↗</a></article>
<figure class="person-photo portrait-full"><img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Sharice_Davids_%28cropped%29.jpg/960px-Sharice_Davids_%28cropped%29.jpg" alt="Sharice Davids in her official congressional portrait" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Sharice Davids · U.S. House Office of Photography, 2019 · Public domain</figcaption></figure><article><label>U.S. REPRESENTATIVE</label><h3>Sharice Davids</h3><b>Ho-Chunk Nation</b><p>A member of the Ho-Chunk Nation representing Kansas in the U.S. House of Representatives. Before entering Congress, she worked as an attorney and competed in mixed martial arts. Her career crosses public service, law, and sport.</p><p class="ru">Шарис Дэвидс — представительница Ho-Chunk Nation и член Палаты представителей США от Канзаса. До политической карьеры она работала юристом и занималась смешанными единоборствами.</p><a href="https://www.ncai.org/news/seven120thcongress" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NCAI · 120th Congress ↗</a></article>
<figure class="person-photo portrait-full"><img src="https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Mary_Peltola_Congressional_Member_Portrait_%282%29_%28cropped%29.jpeg/960px-Mary_Peltola_Congressional_Member_Portrait_%282%29_%28cropped%29.jpeg" alt="Mary Peltola in her official congressional portrait" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Mary Peltola · U.S. House Creative Services, 2022 · Public domain</figcaption></figure><article><label>U.S. SENATOR-ELECT · ALASKA</label><h3>Mary Peltola</h3><b>Yup’ik</b><p>Mary Peltola, a Yup’ik leader from Alaska, was elected to the U.S. Senate in 2026. She previously served in the U.S. House of Representatives and became the first Alaska Native elected to Congress. Her career brings Alaska Native perspectives into national debates.</p><p class="ru">Мэри Пелтола — представительница народа юпик из Аляски. В 2026 году избрана в Сенат США; ранее работала в Палате представителей и стала первой коренной жительницей Аляски, избранной в Конгресс.</p><a href="https://www.ncai.org/news/seven120thcongress" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NCAI · 120th Congress ↗</a></article>

<figure class="person-photo portrait-full"><img src="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Brecheen.jpg" alt="Josh Brecheen in a portrait photograph" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Josh Brecheen · Oklahoma Legislative Service Bureau · Public domain</figcaption></figure><article><label>U.S. REPRESENTATIVE · OKLAHOMA</label><h3>Josh Brecheen</h3><b>Choctaw Nation</b><p>An enrolled citizen of the Choctaw Nation, Josh Brecheen represents Oklahoma in the U.S. House of Representatives. Before Congress, he served in the Oklahoma State Senate and worked as a rancher and small-business owner. He was re-elected to the 120th Congress.</p><p class="ru">Джош Бричин — гражданин Choctaw Nation и член Палаты представителей США от Оклахомы. До избрания в Конгресс работал в Сенате штата Оклахома, занимался фермерским хозяйством и малым бизнесом.</p><a href="https://history.house.gov/People/Detail/25769822940" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. House · Biographical Directory ↗</a><a href="https://www.ncai.org/news/seven120thcongress" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NCAI · 120th Congress ↗</a></article>
<figure class="person-photo portrait-full"><img src="https://nyassembly.gov/write/upload/member_files/037/header_headshot/037.png?hhst=1734551758" alt="Claire Valdez in her official New York State Assembly portrait" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Claire Valdez · Official New York State Assembly portrait · New York State Assembly</figcaption></figure><article><label>U.S. REPRESENTATIVE-ELECT · NEW YORK</label><h3>Claire Valdez</h3><b>Ysleta del Sur Pueblo</b><p>Elected to represent New York’s 7th Congressional District, Claire Valdez is a member of Ysleta del Sur Pueblo. Before her election to Congress, she served in the New York State Assembly and worked as a union organizer with UAW Local 2110 at Columbia University.</p><p class="ru">Клэр Вальдес, гражданка Ysleta del Sur Pueblo, избрана в Палату представителей США от 7-го округа Нью-Йорка. До этого она работала в Ассамблее штата Нью-Йорк и занималась профсоюзной организацией.</p><a href="https://nyassembly.gov/mem/?ad=037&sh=bio" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">New York State Assembly · Biography ↗</a><a href="https://www.ncai.org/news/seven120thcongress" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NCAI · 120th Congress ↗</a></article>
<figure class="person-photo portrait-full"><img src="https://www.michigancapitolconfidential.com/media/images/2023/Sen%20Sean%20McCann%201.jpg" alt="Sean McCann in his Michigan State Senate portrait" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Sean McCann · Michigan State Senate portrait · Image published by Michigan Capitol Confidential</figcaption></figure><article><label>U.S. REPRESENTATIVE-ELECT · MICHIGAN</label><h3>Sean McCann</h3><b>Citizen Potawatomi Nation</b><p>Elected to represent Michigan’s 4th Congressional District, Sean McCann is a citizen of the Citizen Potawatomi Nation. He previously served in the Michigan House of Representatives and State Senate. His election makes him the first Native American elected to represent Michigan in Congress.</p><p class="ru">Шон Макканн, гражданин Citizen Potawatomi Nation, избран в Палату представителей США от 4-го округа Мичигана. Ранее он работал в Палате представителей и Сенате штата Мичиган. Он стал первым представителем коренных народов, избранным в Конгресс от Мичигана.</p><a href="https://www.michiganpublic.org/politics-government/2026-11-12/sean-mccann-michigan-4th-congressional-district" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Michigan Public · Profile and interview ↗</a><a href="https://www.ncai.org/news/seven120thcongress" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NCAI · 120th Congress ↗</a></article>
</div><div class="profiles-list" aria-label="Leaders of sovereign Tribal Nations">
<article><img class="profile-image" src="https://www.navajonationcouncil.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/10/CRYSTALYNE--231x300.jpg" alt="Crystalyne Curley in her official Navajo Nation Council portrait" loading="lazy"><p class="image-credit">Crystalyne Curley · Navajo Nation Council official portrait</p><label>TRIBAL GOVERNMENT · NAVAJO NATION</label><h3>Crystalyne Curley</h3><b>Diné (Navajo Nation)</b><p>A leading figure in Navajo Nation public service. Elected to the Navajo Nation Council in 2022, she became the first woman elected Speaker of the Council in 2023. Her work has included water rights, community services, voting rights, and the responsibilities of Navajo self-government.</p><a href="https://www.navajonationcouncil.org/speaker-crystalyne-curley-biography/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Navajo Nation Council · Official biography ↗</a></article>
<article><label>TRIBAL GOVERNMENT · SENECA NATION</label><h3>Tina Abrams</h3><b>Seneca Nation</b><p>A longtime Seneca Nation leader who was first elected to the Nation’s Council in 2001 and became the first woman appointed legislative Co-Chair in 2012. Her public service has included oversight of Nation enterprises and advocacy on policies affecting Native communities.</p><a href="https://www.cattco.gov/news/2025-brooks-patterson-community-leadership-award-tina-abrams-15663" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cattaraugus County · Community leadership profile ↗</a></article>
<article><img class="profile-image" src="https://www.cherokee.gov/media/rbxlidt2/exec_photo_hoskin_chuck.jpg?format=webp&quality=75&width=1000" alt="Chuck Hoskin Jr. in his official Cherokee Nation portrait" loading="lazy"><p class="image-credit">Chuck Hoskin Jr. · Cherokee Nation official portrait</p><label>TRIBAL GOVERNMENT · CHEROKEE NATION</label><h3>Chuck Hoskin Jr.</h3><b>Cherokee Nation</b><p>Principal Chief of the Cherokee Nation, re-elected in 2023. His administration has expanded Cherokee language and cultural preservation, workforce training, education, housing, health programs, and protections for natural resources.</p><a href="https://www.cherokee.gov/our-government/executive-branch/principal-chief-chuck-hoskin-jr/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Cherokee Nation · Official biography ↗</a></article>
</div><p class="status">Election status matters: Peggy Flanagan and Mary Peltola are senators-elect; Claire Valdez and Sean McCann are representatives-elect. Their terms begin in January 2027. Tom Cole, Josh Brecheen, and Sharice Davids are returning representatives. This page reflects the situation as of November 21, 2026.</p><p class="source-line"><a href="https://www.ncai.org/news/seven120thcongress" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">National Congress of American Indians · November 17, 2026: Seven Native members of the 120th Congress ↗</a></p></section>

<section class="closing"><p class="eyebrow">THE STORY CONTINUES</p><h2>Not a vanished people. Not a closed chapter.</h2><p>These biographies are an introduction, not a complete roll of achievement. Many more scientists, teachers, entrepreneurs, judges, athletes, artists, veterans, language activists, and Tribal leaders deserve to be known. We will keep expanding this collection with Native-led sources and the voices of the nations themselves.</p><p class="ru">Эти биографии — только начало, а не полный перечень достижений. Есть ещё множество учёных, педагогов, предпринимателей, судей, спортсменов, художников, ветеранов, активистов языкового возрождения и руководителей племенных наций, чьи истории заслуживают внимания.</p><a class="return" href="/american-english-method/learn/history-heritage/native-peoples/">Return to Indigenous Peoples of North America ↗</a></section>
